<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    The styles every screen gets — step 22 / 7b, §5c item 10.

    ⚠⚠ **THIS IS WHERE MAUI WEARS THE PORTAL'S COLOURS.** Matt, first hand-run 2026-08-18: *"Colours,
    does not seem to work on MAUI it does on the webtill."* He was right, and the reason was simple:
    the plumbing has been finished for ages — the portal assigns a scheme, the backend resolves it,
    `Theming.Apply` writes the seven `Theme*` keys into `Application.Resources` on start-up and on the
    60-second cadence — but **only two things in the whole app read one of those keys**. A scheme
    moved a page background and the basket rows, and nothing else.

    ⚠⚠ **IMPLICIT STYLES, DELIBERATELY, AND THIS IS THE ARGUMENT.** The alternative was to set the
    colour of every label on seventeen pages by hand: more edits, more places to be wrong, and it
    leaves the NEXT screen unthemed by default. The web till does not do it that way either — it
    defines seven CSS custom properties and lets ~92 rules consume them. This file is that stylesheet.

    ⚠⚠ **INK AND SURFACE MOVE TOGETHER OR NOT AT ALL.** Setting a text colour without owning the colour
    behind it is how Store Information shipped unreadable (1.74.0). So the rules below come in pairs: a
    surface for pages, dialogs and inputs; ink for the text on them; accent with accent-ink for buttons.
    **A scheme that sets a dark surface therefore also gets light ink** — and one that sets neither is
    left at the stock palette exactly, because `Theming.Apply` restores every slot it was not given.
    **Unthemed looks like today**, with the one deliberate exception noted on `Button`.

    ⚠ `DynamicResource`, NEVER `StaticResource`, for a slot — `StaticResource` resolves once at parse
    time, so a page built before a scheme arrived would keep the old palette while later pages changed.
    Half a themed app is worse than none. `XamlResourceTests` fails on both mistakes: a key that does
    not exist, and a slot reached statically.
-->
<?xaml-comp compile="true" ?>
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml">

    <!-- ══ implicit: everything, unless a control says otherwise ══════════════════════════════ -->

    <!--
        ⚠ EVERY PAGE OWNS ITS BACKGROUND. Without this a themed page shows the platform's default
        behind themed text — the unreadable pairing, and on a dark-mode device near-black on near-black.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="ContentPage" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- ⚠ The other half of the pair. Stock `ThemeInk` is #272643, close to the platform default, so
         an unthemed till looks unchanged. -->
    <Style TargetType="Label" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInk}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- ⚠ TYPED TEXT NEEDS BOTH TOO. Left alone under a dark scheme an entry draws dark ink in a
         light box on a dark page, which reads as a rendering fault rather than a theme. -->
    <Style TargetType="Entry" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInk}" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeSurface2}" />
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="Editor" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInk}" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeSurface2}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ⚠⚠ BUTTONS ARE THE MOST VISIBLE SIGNAL THAT A SCHEME ARRIVED, and this is the one rule here
        that changes an UNTHEMED till's appearance: buttons were the platform's default grey and are
        now the stock accent (#2c698d) with white on top.

        ⚠ Deliberate rather than a side effect — `Colors.xaml` records that those values **are** the
        stock theme and that the accent is the web till's own. Matt, 2026-08-17: *"MAUI looks nothing
        like the webtill."* This is part of closing that, and `ThemeAccentInk` is what keeps the text
        readable on a dark accent.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="Button" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeAccent}" />
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeAccentInk}" />
        <!-- ⚠⚠ THE EDGE IS WHAT MAKES A BUTTON EXIST ON A DARK SURFACE (2026-08-19). Matt: *"you
             cannot see the button when the dark theme was set."* The measured arithmetic: the live
             accent (#337061) on the dark surface (#161d26) is 2.94:1 — under the 3:1 floor for a
             SHAPE — while the white label on it is 5.8:1. So the text read and the button did not: a
             column of borderless blocks melting into the page. `ThemeLine` is a slot the live scheme
             actually sets, and the web till draws its non-primary controls with exactly this edge. -->
        <Setter Property="BorderColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeLine}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderWidth" Value="1" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ⚠ THE TYPES WITH NO STYLE AT ALL draw from the PLATFORM — i.e. from the OS light/dark switch,
        never from a custom scheme's surfaces (2026-08-19 audit). Ink and surface as a pair, exactly as
        the Entry rule above and for its reason. The reachable item editor's tax-band and category
        Pickers, the report screen's date pickers and the inventory SearchBar were all in this set.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="Picker" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInk}" />
        <Setter Property="TitleColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInkMuted}" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeSurface2}" />
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="DatePicker" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInk}" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeSurface2}" />
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="SearchBar" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInk}" />
        <Setter Property="PlaceholderColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInkMuted}" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeSurface2}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ⚠⚠ A BARE `Frame` RENDERS WHITE, WHATEVER THE THEME SAYS — found by Matt on 1.99.0
        (2026-08-19): *"In inventory, is each line a white pill? … I do not think its the themeing, I
        think its what was already there that is causing problems?"* He was right on both counts. The
        inventory rows are Frames that predate theming and never named a colour, so they never went
        through it: the LABELS on them followed the global Label style to `ThemeInk` — near-white under
        a dark scheme — while the pill under them stayed platform-white. White text on a white pill.

        ⚠ A STYLE, NOT A PER-SCREEN PATCH, for the same reason the Label/Entry/Editor rules above are
        styles: the next Frame anybody adds must be theme-aware without them knowing this story.
        `Surface2` is the raised-card colour (the same slot dialogs and entries sit on), and the border
        takes `ThemeLine` so the card still reads as a card when surface and surface2 are close.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="Frame" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeSurface2}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeLine}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ⚠ THE OFF SWITCH WAS INVISIBLE UNDER A DARK SCHEME — a dark thumb on a dark page. The switches
        arrived in 1.99.0 (§5c item 9) with no colour set, which made them the newest instance of the
        exact fault they shared a screen with. `ThemeInk` for the thumb keeps it visible in both
        modes: near-white on a dark surface, near-black on a light one.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="Switch" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="OnColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeAccent}" />
        <Setter Property="ThumbColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInk}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- ══ keyed: asked for by name ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!--
        ⚠⚠ THESE TWO WERE REFERENCED TWENTY TIMES AND DEFINED NOWHERE until 2026-08-18. Every basket
        row label carries `Style="{DynamicResource ListItemDetailTextStyle}"` and there was no such key
        — `DynamicResource` to a missing key **applies nothing, silently**: clean build, clean XamlC,
        screen renders with platform defaults. `XamlResourceTests` now fails on it.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ListItemTextStyle" TargetType="Label">
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInk}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalTextAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ⚠⚠ `ThemeInk`, **NOT** `ThemeInkMuted`, and that is deliberate. "Detail" here means *the rest of
        the row*, not *less important*: the price and the VAT column are on it. Muting the price of an
        item is the fault that made Store Information unreadable, and on the basket it would mute the
        numbers the customer is being charged.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ListItemDetailTextStyle" TargetType="Label">
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInk}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalTextAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

    <!-- ⚠ For genuine asides — hints, empty-state notes, "(waiting to send)". The one muted style. -->
    <Style x:Key="MutedTextStyle" TargetType="Label">
        <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInkMuted}" />
    </Style>


    <!-- ⚠⚠ THE SHELL CHROME — WP-T1 T1.1, 2026-08-19. `AppShell.xaml` was BARE, so the tab bar was the
         one surface in the app that followed no scheme at all: the first thing an operator meets, in
         platform default, on a till themed everywhere else. Matt: *"Changing the theme in the portal
         needs to be consistent across the web and MAUI till."*
         ⚠ Accent ground, AccentInk on it — the same pairing the web till's band uses, and now the same
         pairing T1.2 guarantees is legible even when a shop sets only one of the two. -->
    <Style TargetType="Shell" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="Shell.BackgroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeAccent}"/>
        <Setter Property="Shell.ForegroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeAccentInk}"/>
        <Setter Property="Shell.TitleColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeAccentInk}"/>
        <Setter Property="Shell.DisabledColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInkMuted}"/>
        <Setter Property="Shell.UnselectedColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInkMuted}"/>
        <Setter Property="Shell.TabBarBackgroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeAccent}"/>
        <Setter Property="Shell.TabBarForegroundColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeAccentInk}"/>
        <Setter Property="Shell.TabBarTitleColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeAccentInk}"/>
        <Setter Property="Shell.TabBarUnselectedColor" Value="{DynamicResource ThemeInkMuted}"/>
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
